# SECURITY — Capability Taxonomy, Permission Token Model & Op Lookup Matrix

File: openOODA/SECURITY.oot
Logline: Unified capability security specification: permission token taxonomy, DINNER function contract, and runtime op lookup tables.
Setup: Capability tokens gate all side effects; verified at compile-time and runtime sealed.
Beats:
  1. Core Principles & Permission Token Model
  2. Granular vs Broad Capability Catalog
  3. DINNER CAPs Section Specification & Signatures
  4. Capability Op Lookup Table (Check -> Emit -> Runtime)
  5. CAP-G3 Residuals & Ambient Exclusions
  6. Arg-Flow Rules (F01) & Runtime Seal Verification

## 1. Core Principles & Permission Token Model

Rule of Least Privilege:
  Functions receive only the specific token(s) their operations require.
  Static analysis checks calls at compile time (`oodac/check_caps.oo`).
  Process-local token values are verified at native runtime (`runtime/chs_rt_*.c`).
  Missing, forged, or zero tokens fail closed immediately (`ERR\tcap\t...`).

Permission Token Taxonomy:
  1. &ProcessCap
     - Granular process lifecycle token.
     - Controls `sys_exec`, `process_exec`, `sys_spawn`, `sys_wait`, `sys_kill`.
     - Executes child subprocesses with filtered environment (`OODA_*`, `OO_*` + minimal PATH).
  2. &FsReadCap
     - Granular read-only filesystem token.
     - Controls `read_file`, `path_exists`, `file_size`.
     - Read operations obey directory confinement (`OODA_FS_READDIR`).
  3. &FsWriteCap
     - Granular write filesystem token.
     - Controls `write_file`.
     - Write operations strictly obey `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` sandbox overlay (fail-closed if unset).
     - `ooda fix` names that env in one deny line. Unset is still deny, not getcwd.
  4. &SysCap
     - Legacy broad operating system token.
     - Supersedes `&ProcessCap` for backward compatibility on `sys_exec`.
     - Exclusively required for OS primitives: `python_embed`, `sys_epoll_create`, `sys_inotify_init`, `sys_prctl`, async join.
  5. &NetCap
     - Legacy broad networking token.
     - Supersedes granular net tokens (`&HttpCap`, `&TcpCap`, `&UdpCap`, `&BindCap`).
     - Exclusively required for raw network access: `sock_raw`.

## 2. Granular vs Broad Capability Catalog

Full Catalog:
  - FsReadCap: read_file, path_exists, file_size (reads; obeys OODA_FS_READDIR).
  - FsWriteCap: write_file (writes; obeys OODA_FS_WRITEDIR).
  - FsCap: legacy broad filesystem token (supersedes FsReadCap / FsWriteCap).
  - HttpCap: fetch (HTTP/1.0 GET client).
  - TcpCap: tcp_connect, tcp_read, tcp_write, tcp_close, tls_connect.
  - UdpCap: bind_udp, udp_recv, resolve_ipv4 (DNS queries).
  - BindCap: tcp_bind (listening sockets).
  - NetCap: legacy broad network token (supersedes net tokens; required for sock_raw).
  - ProcessCap: sys_exec, process_exec, sys_spawn, sys_wait, sys_kill.
  - SysCap: legacy broad OS token (python_embed, epoll, inotify, prctl, async join, landlock_restrict, rlimit_set_*).
  - EnvCap: env_get (restricted to OODA_* and OO_* keys; raw host getenv denied).
  - TimeCap: now_ms, sleep_ms (system clock and monotonic delays).
  - RandCap: random, seed (entropy extraction and PRNG seeding).
  - AllocCap: alloc_bytes, free_bytes, realloc (heap allocation and quota tracking).
  - ThreadCap: mutex_lock, mutex_unlock, thread_spawn, thread_join, channel_new, channel_send, channel_recv.
  - GpuCap: gpu_launch (CPU/noop path is product; device shaders fail closed).
  - FfiCap / UnsafeFFICap: C bindings and host dynamic library loading (`oo_dlopen`).

## 3. DINNER CAPs Section Specification & Signatures

The `// ## CAPs` header provides a machine-readable, auditable bridge between function signatures and runtime gates.

Format Specification:
  // ## CAPs
  //
  //   <CapName>:    <one-line purpose description>
  //
  //   (Only list caps declared in the function signature.)

Rules & Integration:
  - 1:1 Signature Mirror: Every `&CapName` parameter in `pub fn main(...)` or private functions must appear in the section.
  - Granular First: Prefer `&FsReadCap` over `&FsCap`, `&ProcessCap` over `&SysCap`, `&HttpCap` over `&NetCap`.
  - No Ambient Minting: Helper functions receive tokens passed down from `main`; helpers cannot mint tokens.
  - Zero Tolerance: Phantom caps (listed but unused), unlisted caps, or name mismatch fails compilation and audit linter.
  - Ordering Standard: Process, Fs, Env, Net, Time, Rand, Alloc, Thread, Gpu, Ffi.

Example:
  pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs_r: &FsReadCap, fs_w: &FsWriteCap, env: &EnvCap) {
  // ## CAPs
  //   ProcessCap:    spawn child processes and await exit status
  //   FsReadCap:     load configuration files and fixture inputs
  //   FsWriteCap:    persist build artifacts under OODA_FS_WRITEDIR
  //   EnvCap:        read OODA_* configuration variables

## 4. Capability Op Lookup Table

| Op | Required Cap | Check (`check_caps`) | Emit Lower (`c_emit_lower`) | Runtime Symbol / File | Product Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `read_file` | `&FsReadCap` (or `&FsCap`) | sealed free call | `oo_read_file(cap, path)` | `oo_cap_require_fsread` (`chs_rt_fs.c`) | Real (CAP-G2) |
| `write_file` | `&FsWriteCap` (or `&FsCap`) | sealed free call | `oo_write_file(cap, path, str)`| `oo_cap_require_fswrite` + `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` | Real (CAP-G2) |
| `path_exists` | `&FsReadCap` (or `&FsCap`) | sealed free call | `oo_path_exists(cap, path)` | `oo_cap_require_fsread` (`chs_rt_fs.c`) | Real (CAP-G2) |
| `file_size` | `&FsReadCap` (or `&FsCap`) | sealed free call | `oo_file_size(cap, path)` | `oo_cap_require_fsread` (`chs_rt_fs.c`) | Real (CAP-G2) |
| `sys_exec` | `&ProcessCap` (or `&SysCap`) | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_sys_exec` / `oo_sys_exec1` | `oo_cap_require_process` (`chs_rt_sys.c`) | Real (CAP-G4) |
| `sys_spawn` / `wait` / `kill` | `&ProcessCap` (or `&SysCap`) | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_sys_spawn` / `wait` / `kill` | `oo_cap_require_process` | Fail-closed Residual |
| `env_get` | `&EnvCap` | sealed free call | `oo_env_get(cap, key)` | `oo_process_policy_getenv` (`OODA_`/`OO_`) | Real |
| `fetch` | `&HttpCap` (or `&NetCap`) | sealed free call | `oo_fetch(cap, url)` | `oo_cap_require_http` (`chs_rt_sys.c`) | Real (HTTP/1.0 GET) |
| `tcp_connect` | `&TcpCap` (or `&NetCap`) | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_tcp_connect(cap, h, p)` | `oo_cap_require_tcp` (`chs_rt_netfloor.c`) | Real (M162/M166) |
| `tcp_bind` | `&BindCap` (or `&NetCap`) | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_tcp_bind(cap, port)` | `oo_cap_require_bind` (`chs_rt_netfloor.c`)| Real (M162/M166) |
| `tcp_read` / `write` / `close` | `&TcpCap` (or `&NetCap`) | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_tcp_read` / `write` / `close` | `oo_cap_require_tcp` (`chs_rt_netfloor.c`) | Real (M166) |
| `bind_udp` / `udp_recv` | `&UdpCap` (or `&NetCap`) | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_bind_udp` / `oo_udp_recv` | `oo_cap_require_udp` (`chs_rt_netfloor.c`) | Real (M166) |
| `tls_connect` | `&TcpCap` (or `&NetCap`) | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_tls_connect(cap, h, p)` | `chs_rt_tls.c` (OpenSSL or residual) | Real / Residual |
| `sock_raw` | `&NetCap` only | sealed free call | `oo_sock_raw(cap, ...)` | `oo_cap_require_net` -> always Err | Fail-closed Residual |
| `now_ms` / `sleep_ms` | `&TimeCap` | sealed free call | `oo_now_ms` / `oo_sleep_ms` | `chs_rt_sys.c` (`CLOCK_REALTIME`, nanosleep) | Real (M12) |
| `random` / `seed` | `&RandCap` | sealed free call | `oo_random` / `oo_seed` | `chs_rt_time_rand.c` (host entropy / LCG) | Real (M12) |
| `alloc_bytes` / `free_bytes` | `&AllocCap` | sealed free call | `oo_alloc_bytes` / `oo_free` | `chs_rt_alloc.c` (smoke quota tokens) | Real (M17/M166) |
| `mutex_lock` / `unlock` | `&ThreadCap` | sealed free call | `oo_mutex_lock` / `unlock` | `chs_rt_libfloor.c` (pthread mutex) | Real (M162) |
| `thread_spawn` / `join` | `&ThreadCap` | sealed free call | `oo_thread_spawn` / `join` | `chs_rt_thread.c` (pthread joinable slots)| Real (M163) |
| `channel_new` / `send` / `recv` | `&ThreadCap` | sealed free call | `oo_channel_new` / `send` / `recv`| `chs_rt_channel.c` (bounded string queue)| Real (M164) |
| `gpu_launch` | `&GpuCap` | sealed free call | `oo_gpu_launch(cap, ...)` | `chs_rt_sys.c` (CPU/noop: Ok; shader: Err) | Path-A Real (M165) |
| `sys_epoll_create` / `prctl` | `&SysCap` | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_sys_epoll` / `oo_sys_prctl` | `chs_rt_sys.c` -> Err residual | Fail-closed Residual |
| `python_embed` | `&SysCap` only | sealed free call + arg-flow | `oo_python_embed` | `chs_rt_sys.c` -> Err residual | Fail-closed Residual |

## 5. CAP-G3 Residuals & Ambient Exclusions

CAP-G3 Residual-Only Capabilities:
  Accepted as valid type tokens by compiler, granted stubs in runtime, but possess no lowered sealed ops:
  - &SyncCap, &MemCap, &AudioCap, &CameraCap, &UsbCap, &HidCap, &WindowCap, &FrameCap, &AuditCap, &SignCap, &HitlCap.
  - Forged or direct calls fail closed. No ambient hardware/UI access is claimed or permitted.

Ambient Operations (No Token Required):
  - `process_exit(code)`: Terminates process immediately (standard exit).
  - `list_new`, `list_push`, string concatenation: Memory runtime primitives.
  - Static Taint (`SECRET:`): Compiler taint tracking for secret variables; distinct from dynamic capability tokens.

## 6. Arg-Flow Rules (F01) & Runtime Seal Verification

Arg-Flow Enforcement (F01):
  - Every sealed free call or method call (`fs.read_file(...)`) must pass a capability identifier as its first argument.
  - The argument must resolve to a valid parameter declared in the enclosing function signature.
  - Passing expressions, non-cap variables, or mismatched types results in a compile-time rejection.

Runtime Seal Architecture:
  - Canonical implementation: `STATIC_CAPS.oot`.
  - Capability tokens compile to 64-bit integer handles initialized by `oo_cap_grant_*` at program entry.
  - Each lowered runtime operation checks its token against process-local tables via `oo_cap_require_*`.
  - Hardened Zero Trust: Child processes invoked via `sys_exec` run with scrubbed environments (`OODA_`/`OO_` allowlist).
  - Filesystem sandboxing: `write_file` enforces strict path resolution within `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.

Also see:
  - `openOODA/RULES.oot` — Repository law and capability hygiene (§1.4)
  - `openOODA/WORKFLOW.oot` — OODA lifecycle, swarm coordination, and hostile probing
  - This file is the capability matrix. Residual wants live in ROADMAP.oot.
